\section{Checking terminal support}

On Windows, listing tutorials and running editor steps need no POSIX
terminal modules.  Importing those modules only at their shell call sites
keeps the package usable by a host CLI on either platform.  Both shell
entry points check this capability before doing any setup or checking for
a TTY: a Windows terminal can be a TTY while still lacking PTY support.

<<import the POSIX terminal modules>>=
import fcntl
import pty
import termios
import tty
@

Three callers need to know whether those imports would succeed, and two
of them must not pay for the imports themselves.  The command-line
front end asks while it builds its help text, which happens on every
invocation of a host application, and the runner asks before it creates
a run.  We therefore answer the question by \emph{finding} the modules
rather than importing them: [[find_spec]] locates a module without
executing it, so on a POSIX system the answer costs a few directory
lookups and leaves [[pty]] and [[termios]] unloaded.  It also reports a
module that [[sys.modules]] maps to [[None]] as missing, which is how
our tests model an interpreter without these modules.

<<functions>>=
def posix_terminal_available() -> bool:
    """Return whether PTY-backed shell sessions can run here."""
    <<import the module finder>>
    return all(
        importlib.util.find_spec(name) is not None
        for name in POSIX_TERMINAL_MODULES
    )
<<import the module finder>>=
import importlib.util
<<constants>>=
POSIX_TERMINAL_MODULES = ("fcntl", "pty", "termios", "tty")
@

The shell entry points use the same predicate, so the early answer and
the answer at the point of use cannot disagree.

<<functions>>=
def require_posix_terminal() -> None:
    """Explain how to run shell steps when POSIX support is unavailable."""
    if not posix_terminal_available():
        raise RuntimeError(POSIX_TERMINAL_HINT)
<<constants>>=
POSIX_TERMINAL_HINT = (
    "Interactive tutorials need a POSIX terminal (pty, termios); "
    "on Windows run them inside WSL."
)
