@

Copying once is not enough, because learners resize their terminals.
The kernel announces a resize with [[SIGWINCH]], but only to the
processes of the \emph{real} terminal, which means to us and not to the
shell inside the PTY.  So while a session is relayed we catch the signal
and copy the size again.  That single [[ioctl]] is all the forwarding
that is needed: changing a PTY's size makes the kernel send [[SIGWINCH]]
to the PTY's own foreground process group, so bash, or whatever
full-screen program the learner started, redraws by itself.

We install the handler \emph{before} the first copy, so a resize that
lands between the two is not lost, and we restore the previous handler
afterwards, because a host application may have one of its own.
[[signal.signal]] only works in the main thread; a host that runs a
tutorial from another thread gets the initial size and no live resizing,
which is better than refusing to run.  The relay loop needs no change:
Python retries an interrupted [[select]] once the handler has returned.

<<functions>>=
@contextmanager
def following_window_size(source_fd: int, target_fd: int):
    """Keep ``target_fd``'s window size equal to ``source_fd``'s."""

    def on_resize(signum, frame):
        copy_window_size(source_fd, target_fd)

    try:
        previous = signal.signal(signal.SIGWINCH, on_resize)
    except ValueError:
        previous = None
    try:
        copy_window_size(source_fd, target_fd)
        yield
    finally:
        if previous is not None:
            signal.signal(signal.SIGWINCH, previous)
@

<<test functions>>=
def test_following_window_size_tracks_resizes_and_restores_the_handler(
    terminal_and_pty,
):
    terminal, pty_master = terminal_and_pty
    set_window_size(terminal, 24, 80)
    before = signal.getsignal(signal.SIGWINCH)

    with following_window_size(terminal, pty_master):
        assert window_size(pty_master) == (24, 80)
        set_window_size(terminal, 50, 132)
        os.kill(os.getpid(), signal.SIGWINCH)
        assert window_size(pty_master) == (50, 132)

    assert signal.getsignal(signal.SIGWINCH) == before
@


\section{Launching bash on a configured PTY}

The scripted and interactive shells must start bash in exactly the same PTY
configuration.  A shared helper keeps the controlling-terminal fix in one
place and ensures both paths see the same prompt and shell options.

[[pre_command]] adds one extra requirement for shell-backed steps.  From the
student's perspective, they've already run the setup; they do not know each
step spawns a fresh shell; the tutorial should preserve the illusion of one
continuous terminal session; re-showing setup commands each step would expose
the seams.  Bash rcfiles give us exactly that behaviour: setup commands run
before the first prompt appears, so [[cd]], [[export]], or [[source]] affect
the session the learner sees without leaking into the transcript as authored
commands.

We write that setup script to a temporary rcfile because bash already knows
how to source one script before showing the prompt.  The helper keeps two
important invariants in one place: the script ends with a newline so the last
line is sourced normally, and the temporary file is removed afterwards even if
shell startup fails.

The helper also sizes the PTY, when the caller names a terminal to take
the size from (\cref{shell-window-size}).  It does so on the slave side
and \emph{before} bash starts, so neither readline nor anything a
[[pre_command]] prints ever sees the zero-by-zero default.  Only the
interactive shell passes a terminal.  The scripted shell has no learner
and no terminal to imitate, and leaving its PTY unsized keeps its
transcripts at the eighty-column fallback wherever they are recorded.

<<functions>>=
@contextmanager
def pre_command_rcfile(script: str):
    """Yield a temporary rcfile path containing ``script``."""

    file_handle = tempfile.NamedTemporaryFile(
        mode="w",
        encoding="utf-8",
        suffix=".sh",
        delete=False,
    )
    try:
        text = script if script.endswith("\n") else script + "\n"
        file_handle.write(text)
        file_handle.close()
        yield file_handle.name
    finally:
        try:
            os.unlink(file_handle.name)
        except FileNotFoundError:
            pass
@

<<functions>>=
def spawn_shell_process(
    *,
    workspace: str | Path,
    shell: str = "/bin/bash",
    prompt_name: str = "pytorial",
    env: dict[str, str] | None = None,
    rcfile: str | None = None,
    window_size_from: int | None = None,
) -> tuple[int, subprocess.Popen]:
    """Start bash on a PTY and return the master fd and child process.

    With ``window_size_from``, the PTY starts with that terminal's size.
    """

    <<import the POSIX terminal modules>>
    workspace_path = Path(workspace)
    master_fd, slave_fd = pty.openpty()
    try:
        if window_size_from is not None:
            copy_window_size(window_size_from, slave_fd)
        argv = [shell, "--noprofile"]
        if rcfile is None:
            argv.append("--norc")
        else:
            argv.extend(["--rcfile", rcfile])
        argv.append("-i")
        process = subprocess.Popen(
            argv,
            cwd=workspace_path,
            env=build_shell_environment(env, prompt_name=prompt_name),
            stdin=slave_fd,
            stdout=slave_fd,
            stderr=slave_fd,
            preexec_fn=prepare_child_terminal,
            close_fds=True,
        )
    except Exception:
        os.close(master_fd)
        os.close(slave_fd)
        raise

    os.close(slave_fd)
    return master_fd, process
@


\section{Treating PTY EOF consistently}

Pseudo-terminals signal end-of-file differently from ordinary files.  Some
systems return an empty byte string, while Linux often raises [[EIO]] once
the slave side disappears.  Treating both cases as EOF keeps the relay and
drain loops from spinning forever after the shell exits.

<<functions>>=
def read_pty_output(master_fd: int) -> bytes | None:
    """Read one PTY chunk, or return ``None`` at end-of-file."""

    try:
        data = os.read(master_fd, 4096)
    except OSError as error:
        if error.errno == errno.EIO:
            return None
        raise

    if not data:
        return None
    return data
@

Let's verify that both common PTY EOF signals are normalised to
[[None]].

<<test functions>>=
def test_read_pty_output_treats_eio_as_eof(monkeypatch):
    def raise_eio(master_fd, size):
        raise OSError(errno.EIO, "Input/output error")

    monkeypatch.setattr(os, "read", raise_eio)

    assert read_pty_output(3) is None


def test_read_pty_output_treats_empty_read_as_eof(monkeypatch):
    monkeypatch.setattr(os, "read", lambda master_fd, size: b"")

    assert read_pty_output(3) is None
@


\section{Reading all output from a PTY}

Both the scripted and interactive variants need the same drain logic:
keep reading from the PTY until the shell exits and no more output is
available.

<<functions>>=
def read_transcript(master_fd: int, process: subprocess.Popen) -> str:
    """Drain PTY output until ``process`` exits and the PTY closes."""

    chunks = bytearray()
    master_open = True
    while True:
        readable, _, _ = select.select(
            [master_fd] if master_open else [],
            [],
            [],
            0.05,
        )
        if master_open and master_fd in readable:
            data = read_pty_output(master_fd)
            if data is None:
                master_open = False
            else:
                chunks.extend(data)
                continue

        if process.poll() is not None and not master_open:
            break

    return chunks.decode("utf-8", errors="replace")
@


